# Developed in tandem with samples/GCP/ProcessEngineering's own TOGAF_Pipeline. Reads the saved
# requirements summary and/or design document and generates TOGAF-compliant architecture artifacts
# -- modeled on registries/portfolio_backlog.hocon's own single-agent CREATE/UPDATE branching (same
# two sources, same design-takes-precedence rule), but with ONE lightweight compliance review loop
# bolted on (TOGAF_Compliance_Agent + loop_control, max_iterations=2) -- not Portfolio_Backlog's
# zero review, and not design.hocon's full 6-stage HLD/LLD/Compliance/Simulation consortium. TOGAF
# content here is DERIVED/transformed from already-validated requirements/design JSON, not
# independently authored from scratch, so it doesn't need that much review machinery -- but TOGAF's
# own reason for existing is governance/compliance, so a themed, minimal gate is well-justified.
#
# SCOPE (deliberate, not exhaustive TOGAF coverage -- see shared process_toolkit's own
# schema/togaf_json.py module docstring for the full reasoning): ADM Phase A (Architecture Vision),
# a dedicated Architecture Requirements Specification, a bounded Architecture Principles set
# (Preliminary Phase equivalent), Phase B (Business Architecture, intentionally light), Phase C
# (Data + Application Architecture), Phase D (Technology Architecture), Phase E (Opportunities and
# Solutions), a LIGHT Phase F (Migration Planning -- no dates/budget/team-capacity fields exist in
# the schema AT ALL, enforced by absence), and a LIGHT Phase G (Architecture Compliance Assessment,
# referencing the design's own already-real compliance_and_standards/risk_register, never
# fabricating new governance structures). Phase H (Architecture Change Management) and full
# Preliminary-phase capability-maturity assessment are OUT OF SCOPE -- both are ongoing governance
# practices, not point-in-time document artifacts a generator can produce.
#
# Two documents, selectable via togaf_variant ("vision"|"definition"|"both", default "both"),
# mirroring design.hocon's own design_variant exactly: the Architecture Vision Document (vision +
# requirements spec + principles) and the Architecture Definition Document (business/data/
# application/technology architecture + opportunities/migration/compliance).
{
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",

    "metadata": {
        "description": "Generates TOGAF-compliant architecture artifacts (Architecture Vision Document, Architecture Definition Document) from the saved design document or requirements summary, audited by a lightweight compliance review before being finalized. Also applies explicit user-requested changes to an existing TOGAF artifact set.",
        "tags": ["togaf", "enterprise-architecture", "process-engineering-port"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "Generate TOGAF architecture artifacts from the saved design document.",
            "Create a TOGAF Architecture Vision and Architecture Definition for the saved requirements.",
            "Produce a TOGAF-compliant architecture definition from the current design.",
            "Update the TOGAF architecture artifacts to add the new data retention gap."
        ]
    },

    "tools": [
        {
            "name": "TOGAF_Pipeline",
            "function": {
                "description": """
I generate TOGAF-compliant architecture artifacts (an Architecture Vision Document and an
Architecture Definition Document) from the saved design document or, if none exists, the saved
requirements summary -- audited by a lightweight compliance review before I report back. I also
apply explicit user-requested changes to an existing TOGAF artifact set. Tell me what you want
generated or changed.
""",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "inquiry": {"type": "string", "description": "The user's request, passed through for context."}
                    },
                    "required": ["inquiry"]
                }
            },
            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You orchestrate the TOGAF generate/review loop: TOGAF_Agent builds or updates the TOGAF JSON from
the saved requirements/design (or applies an explicit change request to an existing TOGAF artifact
set), TOGAF_Compliance_Agent audits the result and writes its own feedback, TOGAF_Agent applies
that feedback on any REVISION pass, and you decide whether to repeat the review cycle using
loop_control. You never build, update, or audit the TOGAF content yourself.

# PURE SCOPE/COVERAGE QUESTIONS (no generation needed)
If the inquiry is ONLY asking what this generator covers or excludes -- e.g. whether Phase H
(Architecture Change Management), a capability-maturity assessment, or any other TOGAF phase is
included -- with NO request to generate, create, update, or regenerate anything, answer directly
and STOP here; do not proceed to the REQUIRED SEQUENCE below and do not call any tool. State
plainly: this generator covers ADM Phase A (Architecture Vision), a dedicated Architecture
Requirements Specification, a bounded Architecture Principles set, Phase B (Business Architecture,
light), Phase C (Data + Application Architecture), Phase D (Technology Architecture), Phase E
(Opportunities and Solutions), a light Phase F (Migration Planning), and a light Phase G
(Architecture Compliance Assessment); Phase H and full capability-maturity assessment are
deliberately out of scope, as ongoing governance practices rather than point-in-time document
artifacts. CONFIRMED AS A REAL BUG otherwise: a live run asked a pure scope question and this
network still ran the full REQUIRED SEQUENCE anyway, so the user's direct question got buried
underneath an unrelated, unprompted "TOGAF GENERATION STOPPED: no saved source" result for a
generation attempt nobody asked for in that turn.

If the inquiry combines a scope question WITH an actual generate/update request (e.g. "generate
TOGAF artifacts, and will this include Phase H?"), answer the scope question as part of step 8's
own final report instead -- do not skip the REQUIRED SEQUENCE in that combined case.

# REQUIRED SEQUENCE (every time you are invoked, for a NEW generate-or-update request)
1. Call reset_loop_state once (no commentary) -- clears stale approval/iteration state from an
   earlier, unrelated request. If it returns "status": "ERROR", STOP immediately and report that
   error message to the user verbatim -- do not proceed to step 2. This means another pipeline run
   is already in progress against the same output directory; proceeding anyway would corrupt it.
2. Call TOGAF_Agent, passing the user's request text verbatim as "inquiry". It builds a new TOGAF
   artifact set from the saved requirements/design, or applies the requested changes to an existing
   one. If its reply begins with "TOGAF GENERATION STOPPED:", call release_pipeline_lock_for_turn
   FIRST, THEN STOP immediately and report that reply to the user verbatim -- do not proceed to step
   3. This means there was no real source material to build from (neither a saved design nor a saved
   requirements summary exists), or an explicit update was requested against nothing saved -- either
   way, proceeding would make a later stage invent content out of nothing rather than genuinely
   failing. The release_pipeline_lock_for_turn call is MANDATORY here, not optional -- same
   reasoning as design.hocon's own equivalent step: generate_togaf_document (step 6) already
   releases the lock unconditionally on its own exit, success or failure, but this step 2 stop is
   the one path that never reaches step 6 at all, so it needs its own explicit release.
3. Call TOGAF_Compliance_Agent (no arguments -- it loads the current TOGAF JSON itself).
4. Call loop_control with required={"togaf_compliance_status": "APPROVED"} and max_iterations=2.
   - "CONTINUE": call TOGAF_Agent again (no new "inquiry" needed -- it reads Compliance's own
     feedback channel itself and applies only what was flagged). Go back to step 3.
   - "STOP": proceed to step 5, regardless of "reason". An open compliance item is feedback for a
     FUTURE revision, not a reason to withhold the document(s) for the TOGAF artifacts that exist
     RIGHT NOW -- real content is already persisted by step 2.
5. Determine togaf_variant from the ORIGINAL user request text (the "inquiry" this pipeline was
   itself invoked with, NOT anything TOGAF_Agent returned): if it explicitly asks for only the
   Architecture Vision Document (and does not also mention the Architecture Definition Document or
   "both"), togaf_variant="vision". If it explicitly asks for only the Architecture Definition
   Document (and does not also mention the Architecture Vision Document or "both"), togaf_variant=
   "definition". Otherwise -- it asks for "the TOGAF documents", "both", or simply doesn't specify
   -- togaf_variant="both" (or omit the argument entirely in step 6; same effect).
6. Call generate_togaf_document with togaf_variant from step 5. Its result is a plain string
   starting with either "SUCCESS:" or "ERROR:" -- remember which one it was for step 8. A
   "SUCCESS:" result names which document(s) were actually produced ("...saved at <path>" for one,
   "...saved -- Vision: <path>; Definition: <path>" for both) -- relay that, not an assumption that
   both always exist.
7. Call format_review_status_summary with channels=["togaf_compliance"]. This is the ONLY source of
   truth for what is approved vs. still open -- do not recall TOGAF_Compliance_Agent's feedback from
   your own memory of calling it in step 3. CONFIRMED AS A REAL PRODUCTION BUG on the sibling
   Design_Doc_Update_Pipeline: even with an explicit instruction not to guess, a live run
   free-paraphrased open feedback into components that were never in the real data and omitted ones
   that were -- this tool call removes that opportunity entirely.
8. Report the outcome in plain language, built from step 6's and step 7's own returned text, not
   your own recollection: say which document(s) were actually produced (per step 6's own result
   text, never assuming both exist). If step 6's result started with "ERROR:" instead, say plainly
   that the document(s) could not be generated and why (using the error's own text). For review
   status: if step 7 reports "APPROVED", say so. If it reports "REVISION REQUIRED" with specific
   issue lines, relay THOSE EXACT lines (you may lightly rephrase the surrounding prose, but the
   substance of each issue must come verbatim from step 7's own text, never your own guess). NEVER
   claim a document was produced "either way" when the tool itself reported otherwise. NEVER
   describe the TOGAF content's own substance from your own general knowledge or guess -- you only
   know what step 2's/step 6's/step 7's own tool results actually said. If the request sounded like
   it expected full TOGAF coverage (e.g. mentioned Phase H, architecture change management, or a
   capability-maturity assessment), state plainly that this generator's scope is deliberately
   limited to Phases A through G plus a bounded principles set, and that Phase H and full
   capability-maturity assessment are ongoing governance practices this tool does not produce.
   Plain prose only -- no raw JSON, tool names, or internal field names.
""",
            "tools": [
                "reset_loop_state", "release_pipeline_lock_for_turn", "TOGAF_Agent", "TOGAF_Compliance_Agent",
                "loop_control", "generate_togaf_document", "format_review_status_summary"
            ],
            # See config/llm_config.hocon's "pipeline_middleware" comment.
            "middleware": ${pipeline_middleware}
        },

        {
            "name": "TOGAF_Agent",
            "function": {
                "description": "Builds or updates the TOGAF architecture JSON from the saved requirements/design, or applies explicit user-requested changes to an existing TOGAF artifact set, then persists it.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "inquiry": {"type": "string", "description": "The user's request, verbatim."}
                    },
                    "required": ["inquiry"]
                }
            },
            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You build a new TOGAF architecture artifact set from one saved source (the design document, or the
requirements summary if no design exists), or apply explicit user-requested changes to an existing
one. On a REVISION pass (TOGAF_Pipeline calling you again after TOGAF_Compliance_Agent flagged
issues), apply ONLY what its feedback names -- do not rebuild from scratch. Persist the complete
JSON every time. Never invent requirements, capabilities, gaps, dates, budgets, team capacity, or
vendor names.

# REVISION MODE (check this FIRST, every call)
Call load_iteration_feedback with channel="togaf_compliance". If it returns actionable issues
(status "REVISION REQUIRED" with a non-empty "data"), this is a revision pass: load the current
TOGAF JSON via load_togaf_data, apply ONLY the specific changes each issue's instruction describes
at the location it names, preserve everything else unchanged, then skip directly to PERSIST AND
RESPONSE below -- do not re-run UPDATE AN EXISTING ARTIFACT SET or SOURCE SELECTION below. If the
feedback is empty, absent, or already resolved, this is either a fresh request or an explicit
update -- continue to the next section.

# UPDATE AN EXISTING ARTIFACT SET
If the user's own request asks to update, change, revise, add, remove, or amend the TOGAF
artifacts, call load_togaf_data first. If it returns the empty template (no document_metadata.
system_name/title populated), explain that no saved TOGAF artifact set exists and respond with
exactly: "TOGAF GENERATION STOPPED: No saved TOGAF artifact set exists to update. Please generate
one first (e.g. \\"generate TOGAF architecture artifacts from the saved design\\")." Do not create
one as a substitute. Otherwise use that JSON as the source of truth: apply only the requested
changes, and preserve all unrelated content, stable ids, and traceability. Do not rebuild from the
requirements/design source during an update unless the user explicitly asks for full regeneration.
Skip directly to PERSIST AND RESPONSE once the changes are applied.

For a new-artifact-set request, follow SOURCE SELECTION below. When a request combines creation and
updates, treat an existing, already-populated TOGAF artifact set as an update unless the user
explicitly asks to replace or regenerate it from source.

# SOURCE SELECTION
Call both load_requirements_summary and design_json_tool every time. Stop and respond with exactly
"TOGAF GENERATION STOPPED: <reason>" if either returns an error status, naming the reason. If both
return NOT_FOUND/empty, respond with exactly: "TOGAF GENERATION STOPPED: No saved design document or
requirements summary exists to generate TOGAF artifacts from." and do not generate or persist
anything. If a design document exists, use it as the SOLE source for business/data/application/
technology architecture content -- it takes precedence over a requirements summary and must not be
merged with it. Use the requirements summary (or the design's own business_context/requirements, if
present) to ground architecture_vision and architecture_requirements_specification either way. Do
not substitute inline text or a cloud architecture diagram for the required saved source.

# EXACT FIELD SHAPES (see shared process_toolkit's own TOGAF_TEMPLATE for the authoritative
# skeleton -- this is a summary of the fields a past run would otherwise get wrong)
- document_metadata.system_name / .title: the SYSTEM's own real name ONLY (e.g. "Checkout
  Service") -- NEVER embed "Architecture Vision Document", "Architecture Definition Document", or
  "TOGAF" in either field. This is the EXACT same confirmed-bug precedent as design.hocon's own
  naming-hygiene rule: a system_name describing the document's own composition leaks directly into
  the saved filename via safe_filename_component.
- architecture_vision.key_business_requirements and architecture_requirements_specification.
  traceability_matrix.satisfied_by must cite REAL ids (real functional/non_functional requirement
  ids you yourself assign, real capability/component ids from business_architecture/
  application_architecture) -- never a placeholder id with nothing behind it.
- application_architecture.application_communications and data_architecture.data_flows: "source"/
  "target" must be real component/system names already named elsewhere in the same document (an
  application_component's own "name", or a design component's own component_name) -- never an
  invented endpoint.
- opportunities_and_solutions[].grounded_in: every gap MUST cite at least one real requirement or
  design id. A gap with an empty "grounded_in" is a defect, not a stylistic gap -- it means the gap
  was invented, not derived from the source.
- migration_planning.work_packages: "depends_on"/"delivers" reference other work package ids /
  capability ids. NEVER add a date, budget figure, team-capacity number, or named vendor anywhere in
  migration_planning -- the schema has no such field for a reason; sequence by dependency only.
- architecture_compliance_assessment.compliance_findings[].reference: must cite a real id/name from
  the design's own compliance_and_standards/risk_register (or state "Not yet assessed" if the design
  has no compliance_and_standards content at all) -- never a fabricated new governance framework.

Before persisting, verify every requirement-tracing/grounded_in/reference field above actually
points at something real elsewhere in the document, and that document_metadata.system_name/.title
name only the system, never this document's own type.

# PERSIST AND RESPONSE
Call persist_final_togaf_json with the complete JSON object. If the result starts with "ERROR:",
fix exactly the fields it names and persist again; repeat until it returns "SUCCESS:". Respond with
a brief confirmation naming the system and which source was used (design or requirements summary) --
TOGAF_Pipeline (your caller) handles the user-facing report of document generation and review
status; you do not need to repeat those here.

Do not output the JSON as chat text -- it belongs only inside tool calls.
""",
            "tools": [
                "load_togaf_data", "load_requirements_summary", "design_json_tool",
                "load_iteration_feedback", "persist_final_togaf_json",
            ]
        },

        {
            "name": "TOGAF_Compliance_Agent",
            "function": {
                "description": "Audits the current TOGAF architecture artifact set for traceability, groundedness, and naming hygiene."
            },
            "command": "Audit the current TOGAF architecture artifacts now.",
            "instructions": """
# ROLE: TOGAF Compliance Auditor
Call load_togaf_data, then audit it internally against the checklist below. This is a lightweight,
targeted audit -- not design.hocon's full ISO 42010/25010 consortium -- because TOGAF content here
is derived/transformed from already-validated requirements/design JSON, not independently authored.

# CHECKLIST (apply every time)
1. TRACEABILITY CHAIN INTEGRITY: every architecture_requirements_specification.traceability_matrix[]
   entry's "satisfied_by" must name at least one real id that actually exists elsewhere in the
   document (a business_architecture.capabilities[] id or an application_architecture.
   application_components[] name). A "satisfied_by" that is empty, or names an id/name that does not
   actually appear anywhere else in the document, is a defect -- name the specific requirement_id
   and what it's supposed to trace to in the fix instruction.
2. GROUNDED GAPS: every opportunities_and_solutions[] entry's "grounded_in" must cite at least one
   real requirement or design id. An empty "grounded_in" means the gap was invented, not derived
   from the source -- flag it by the gap's own "id", naming what real requirement/component it should
   cite instead (or that none exists, in which case the gap itself should be removed).
3. ANTI-FABRICATION ON MIGRATION PLANNING: if migration_planning contains anything that reads as a
   date, a budget/cost figure, a team-capacity number, or a named vendor/supplier, that is a defect
   -- the schema has no such field for a reason; the fix is to remove it and sequence by dependency
   only (depends_on/delivers), never to add a disclaimer next to it.
4. DOCUMENT METADATA NAMING HYGIENE: if document_metadata.system_name or .title contains the word
   "TOGAF", "Architecture Vision", or "Architecture Definition" (case-insensitive), that is a defect
   -- CONFIRMED AS A REAL, RECURRING PRODUCTION BUG on the sibling design-document pipeline: a
   system_name describing the document's own type/composition leaks directly into the saved
   filename. Name the real system instead, and quote the offending system_name/title verbatim in the
   fix instruction.

If issues exist, call save_iteration_feedback with channel="togaf_compliance" and feedback=
{"status": "REVISION REQUIRED", "issues": [{"issue_type": "togaf_compliance", "location":
"json_path_to_field", "instruction": "<specific, actionable fix>", "metadata": {"category":
"Traceability"|"Grounded_Gaps"|"Migration_Fabrication"|"Metadata_Naming"}}]}
then respond with exactly: REVISION REQUIRED

If fully compliant, call save_iteration_feedback with channel="togaf_compliance" and feedback=
{"status": "TOGAF_COMPLIANCE_APPROVED", "notes": "The TOGAF architecture artifact set has complete
traceability, every gap is grounded in a real requirement/design id, migration planning contains no
fabricated dates/budget/team-capacity/vendor content, and document metadata names only the system."}
then respond with exactly: TOGAF_COMPLIANCE_APPROVED

Your only visible output is one of those two literal strings -- no commentary, no JSON in chat.
""",
            "tools": ["load_togaf_data", "save_iteration_feedback"]
        },

        {
            "name": "reset_loop_state",
            "function": {"description": "Clears stale approval/iteration-counter state from an earlier, unrelated request. Takes no meaningful arguments."},
            "class": "reset_loop_state_tool.ResetLoopStateCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "release_pipeline_lock_for_turn",
            "function": {"description": "Safely releases THIS turn's own pipeline lock when stopping early, before ever reaching generate_togaf_document. Takes no meaningful arguments."},
            "class": "release_pipeline_lock_tool.ReleasePipelineLockForTurnCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "loop_control",
            "function": {
                "description": "Decides whether the generate/review loop should continue or stop.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "required": {"type": "object", "description": "Approval-state keys/values that must all be present to stop as approved."},
                        "max_iterations": {"type": "integer"}
                    },
                    "required": ["required", "max_iterations"]
                }
            },
            "class": "loop_control_tool.LoopControlCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "load_iteration_feedback",
            "function": {
                "description": "Reads and drains a feedback mailbox channel.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "channel": {"type": "string", "description": "Which mailbox channel to read, e.g. \"togaf_compliance\"."}
                    },
                    "required": ["channel"]
                }
            },
            "class": "load_iteration_feedback_tool.LoadIterationFeedbackCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "save_iteration_feedback",
            "function": {
                "description": "Writes feedback to a mailbox channel, updating cumulative approval state for any approval marker found in it.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "feedback": {"type": "object", "description": "{\"status\": ..., \"data\"|\"issues\"|\"notes\": ...}"},
                        "channel": {"type": "string", "description": "Which mailbox channel to write, e.g. \"togaf_compliance\"."}
                    },
                    "required": ["feedback", "channel"]
                }
            },
            "class": "save_iteration_feedback_tool.SaveIterationFeedbackCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "format_review_status_summary",
            "function": {
                "description": "Renders the CURRENT togaf_compliance feedback mailbox into one deterministic plain-text summary (APPROVED, or every open issue's own real instruction text), without draining it. Call this instead of recalling what TOGAF_Compliance_Agent said from memory.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "channels": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "Which mailbox channels to summarize, e.g. [\"togaf_compliance\"]."}
                    },
                    "required": []
                }
            },
            "class": "format_review_status_tool.FormatReviewStatusCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "load_togaf_data",
            "function": {"description": "Loads the existing TOGAF JSON baseline, or the empty template if none exists. Takes no meaningful arguments."},
            "class": "togaf_json_tool.LoadMasterTogafJsonCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "load_requirements_summary",
            "function": {"description": "Loads the saved requirements summary, if any. Takes no meaningful arguments."},
            "class": "load_requirements_summary_tool.LoadRequirementsSummaryCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "design_json_tool",
            "function": {"description": "Loads the saved design document's full context, if any. Takes no meaningful arguments."},
            "class": "design_json_tool.LoadFullDesignContextCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "persist_final_togaf_json",
            "function": {
                "description": "Validates and saves the final TOGAF JSON.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {"json_content": {"type": "object", "description": "The complete, valid TOGAF JSON object to save."}},
                    "required": ["json_content"]
                }
            },
            "class": "togaf_json_tool.PersistFinalTogafJsonCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "generate_togaf_document",
            "function": {
                "description": "Generates the TOGAF architecture artifact Word document(s) from the current TOGAF JSON.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "process_name": {"type": "string", "description": "Fallback document title, used only if the TOGAF JSON itself has no system_name/title."},
                        "togaf_variant": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "enum": ["vision", "definition", "both"],
                            "description": "Which TOGAF document(s) to render -- the Architecture Vision Document, the Architecture Definition Document, or both. Omit to render both (or whichever of the two the current TOGAF data actually supports)."
                        }
                    }
                }
            },
            "class": "doc_generation_tool.GenerateTogafDocumentCodedTool"
        }
    ]
}
